@* A long message folded. <details> again: it opens with no script, survives a fragment swap, and
   Find in Page reaches inside it in most browsers — none of which a state variable manages.

   Two things the element dictates, and both were got wrong here before:

   Everything that is not the <summary> is hidden while it is closed. So the part that stays on
   screen — the preview — goes INSIDE the trigger, not above it. Putting it above left a bubble
   containing nothing but the words "Show more".

   And the control has to go once the text is shown. There is no "Show less" here: the whole
   point of the control is to reveal the rest, and once it is revealed the row is only in the
   way. The element's own [open] hides it, so there is nothing to keep in step — and the
   summary still holds the preview, so a second click on the text folds it back. *@
<div class="flex w-full max-w-sm flex-col gap-8">
    <Bubble Variant="muted">
        <BubbleContent>How can I help you today?</BubbleContent>
    </Bubble>

    <Bubble Variant="muted" Align="end">
        <BubbleContent>
            <Collapsible Class="group/more">
                <CollapsibleTrigger Class="flex cursor-pointer flex-col gap-2 text-start">
                    <span>
                        The accessibility review found two focus states that were visually too
                        subtle in dark mode. I checked the dialog, menu, and drawer paths because
                        each one renders focusable controls inside a layered surface.
                    </span>
                    <span class="@ButtonVariants.Of("link", "default", "h-auto gap-1 self-start p-0 text-muted-foreground group-open/more:hidden")">
                        Show more
                        <Icon Name="chevron-down" Class="size-4" />
                    </span>
                </CollapsibleTrigger>
                <CollapsibleContent Class="mt-2 flex flex-col gap-2">
                    <p>
                        The dialog and drawer are fine. The menu needs the hover and focus tokens
                        split so keyboard focus stays visible when the pointer is not involved.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        I also recommend keeping the change in the style file instead of the
                        primitive, so the other themes can choose their own focus treatment later.
                    </p>
                </CollapsibleContent>
            </Collapsible>
        </BubbleContent>
    </Bubble>
</div>
